# Interpretation and compilation

## Two semantics

Normal words: compile time appends their **interpretation** semantics. Control words (`if`, `then`) have non-default **compilation** semantics.

## Immediate

```forth
: [print5] ( -- )  5 . ;  immediate
```

During compilation of `: bar … ;`, `[print5]` runs at compile time (interpretation semantics). Mark non-default words with bracket names or `immediate`.

### Early `[IMMEDIATE]` marker

Optional sugar (theForthNet `immediate` package): declare intent at the start of the body:

```forth
: [print5] ( -- )  [immediate]  5 . ;
```

Equivalent to `: [print5] … ; immediate` when supported. **Portable code:** keep trailing `; immediate` unless the target loads the extension. `IMMEDIATE` before `;` inside `: … does> … ;` is ambiguous on some systems — avoid in portable defining words.

## Compile-only

Words like `if` should not run at interpret time — mark `compile-only` to get a warning when interpreted accidentally. Gforth warns on `'` / `[']` for compile-only words too; at interpret time, compile-only words run their compilation semantics (ANS/Gforth conformance).

Document **both** stack effects when compile-time differs from run-time:

```forth
: [print5] ( Compilation: -- ; Run-time: -- )
  ['] . compile, 5 , ;  immediate
```

## State switches

`:` enters compile state; `;` returns to interpret state. Brackets:

- `[` — interpret state (compile-time evaluation inside a definition)
- `]` — compile state

```forth
: demo ( -- )
  [ 5 . ]   \ runs 5 . while compiling demo
;
```

## POSTPONE

Compile the **compilation** semantics of a word:

```forth
: MY-+  ( Compilation: -- ; Run-time: n1 n2 -- n )
  postpone + ;  immediate
```

Document **both** stack effects for immediate defining words. Prefer `postpone` over `[ immediate-word ]` inside definitions — the bracket form is non-standard and fragile on some systems.

Chain example:

```forth
: MY--  ( Compilation: -- ; Run-time: n1 n2 -- n )
  postpone negate  postpone + ;  immediate  compile-only
```

## Execution tokens in meta words

`' word` at interpret time; `['] word` while compiling. Use `postpone` instead of manual `compile,` when wrapping existing words.

## Parsing words

Words like `create`, `'`, `[']` read from the **input stream**, not the stack — hard to call from other words with computed parameters. **Always provide a stack-parameter factor** and implement the parsing word on top with `parse-name` / `parse`.

Do not build new parsing words unless necessary; prefer `( c-addr u )` or stack arguments.

## Interpret-only wordlist (advanced)

`interpretive.fs` (theForthNet) puts helpers in a wordlist searched **first in interpret state only** — same notation at compile vs interpret time without state-smart `'`. Example: interpret-time `' name` vs compile-time `['] name` wrapped as one immediate word.

**Not a standard system** after loading — changes search order with `[` / `]` / `:`. Use only in meta-libraries; do not pull into challenge solutions unless the scaffold already depends on it.

## Recognizers (advanced)

Gforth extends the text interpreter with a recognizer stack (`get-recognizers` / `set-recognizers`). Normal application `.fs` code should not touch this — only meta-tooling and interpreter extensions. Example recognizers: `sources/theforth.net-packages/recognizers/current/`.

See `forth-defining.mdc` for CREATE/DOES> and `forth-control.mdc` for immediate control-flow words.
